## Technical Description

### Field of the Invention

The present disclosure relates generally to semiconductor crystal growth equipment and, more particularly, to systems and methods for storing, charging, weighing, transferring, identifying, and managing recharge tubes used to introduce semiconductor feedstock material into a crystal pulling apparatus, such as a Czochralski crystal puller.

### Background

In semiconductor crystal pulling processes, polycrystalline silicon or other source material may be melted in a crucible and used to grow a single-crystal ingot. During extended or repeated pulling operations, additional feedstock may be supplied to the melt using a recharge tube. A recharge tube may be filled with a controlled quantity of semiconductor feedstock material and subsequently transferred to a crystal puller, where the material is discharged into the crucible.

Conventional handling of recharge tubes may involve manual loading, manual weighing, manual identification, and manual transfer between storage, filling, and process equipment. Such practices may introduce variability, particulate contamination, operator safety concerns, traceability limitations, and inefficiencies in high-volume semiconductor manufacturing. Accordingly, there is a need for an integrated system capable of managing recharge tubes in a controlled, traceable, and automated manner.

### Summary of the Invention

In one embodiment, a system is provided for storing, charging, weighing, transferring, and managing recharge tubes for semiconductor crystal pulling. The system includes a recharge tube storage unit configured to hold a plurality of recharge tubes, a charging station configured to dispense semiconductor feedstock material into selected recharge tubes, a weighing station configured to determine a weight of a recharge tube before, during, or after charging, a transfer mechanism configured to move recharge tubes between stations and/or to a crystal pulling apparatus, and a control system configured to coordinate operation of the storage unit, charging station, weighing station, and transfer mechanism.

In certain embodiments, the system includes an identification and tracking module configured to associate each recharge tube with identification data, process data, weight data, material lot data, destination data, and/or usage history. The control system may determine a target charge mass for each recharge tube, control dispensing of feedstock material based on measured weight, verify that the charged tube satisfies predetermined criteria, and authorize transfer of the recharge tube to a designated crystal puller.

In further embodiments, the system includes environmental control features, such as an enclosed housing, inert gas purge, filtered airflow, particle monitoring, humidity control, and contamination-reducing interfaces. The system may be configured to interface with factory automation, a manufacturing execution system, and/or a crystal puller controller.

### Detailed Description of Exemplary Embodiments

#### 1. System Overview

A recharge tube management system may include one or more of the following functional modules:

1. a recharge tube storage module;
2. a recharge tube charging module;
3. a weighing module;
4. a transfer module;
5. a tube identification and tracking module;
6. a material dispensing and metering module;
7. a control module;
8. an operator interface;
9. a factory automation interface; and
10. a contamination control enclosure.

The modules may be arranged as separate physical stations connected by a transfer mechanism, or may be integrated into a single tool enclosure. In one embodiment, the system is positioned adjacent to one or more semiconductor crystal pulling furnaces. In another embodiment, the system is located in a material preparation area and is configured to deliver charged recharge tubes to one or more crystal pullers using an automated transport device.

The recharge tubes may be configured to contain chunks, pellets, granules, rods, or other forms of semiconductor feedstock material, including silicon, germanium, silicon-germanium, doped silicon, or other crystal growth materials. Each recharge tube may have an upper loading opening and a lower discharge opening, one or both of which may be selectively closed by caps, valves, gates, plugs, shutters, or other closure members.

#### 2. Recharge Tube Storage Module

The storage module may include a rack, carousel, magazine, vertical storage tower, horizontal cassette, indexed storage frame, or other structure configured to receive and support multiple recharge tubes. The storage module may include a plurality of tube receiving positions, each position having mechanical support surfaces configured to hold a recharge tube in a predetermined orientation.

In one embodiment, the storage module stores recharge tubes in a generally vertical orientation. In another embodiment, the tubes are stored horizontally or at an inclined angle. The storage module may include locating features, such as pins, grooves, V-blocks, clamps, tapered guides, or kinematic mounts, to ensure repeatable positioning of each tube.

Each tube receiving position may include one or more sensors configured to detect the presence, orientation, identity, temperature, cleanliness state, cap state, or fill state of a recharge tube. Suitable sensors include optical sensors, proximity sensors, load sensors, RFID readers, barcode scanners, machine vision cameras, capacitive sensors, and mechanical switches.

The storage module may be configured to segregate recharge tubes according to status, such as empty, clean, ready for charging, charged, awaiting transfer, rejected, maintenance due, or returned from a puller. The storage module may include dedicated zones for different statuses, material grades, dopant types, destination pullers, or production lots.

#### 3. Recharge Tube Identification and Tracking

Each recharge tube may include a unique identifier. The identifier may be provided by an RFID tag, barcode, QR code, engraved serial number, optical mark, magnetic tag, memory device, or other machine-readable identification element. The identifier may be located on a tube body, cap, carrier, support collar, or associated handling fixture.

An identification reader may be provided at one or more locations, including the storage module, charging station, weighing station, transfer interface, and crystal puller docking interface. The control system may read the identifier and retrieve or create a tube record associated with the recharge tube.

The tube record may include one or more of:

- tube serial number;
- tube type or capacity;
- current location;
- cleanliness state;
- empty weight or tare weight;
- last measured weight;
- target charge mass;
- actual charge mass;
- feedstock material lot number;
- dopant information;
- charging time and date;
- operator or recipe identification;
- destination crystal puller;
- associated ingot or batch;
- number of usage cycles;
- maintenance history;
- inspection status;
- cap or valve status;
- contamination or particle inspection data; and
- authorization status for use.

The control system may prevent charging or transfer of a recharge tube if the tube record indicates that the tube is expired, improperly cleaned, incompatible with a selected material, assigned to a different process, overweight, underweight, damaged, or otherwise outside of specification.

#### 4. Charging Station

The charging station is configured to introduce semiconductor feedstock material into a selected recharge tube. The charging station may include a feedstock supply hopper, intermediate bin, vibratory feeder, screw feeder, belt feeder, rotary valve, chute, funnel, gate valve, metering container, or robotic scoop mechanism.

In one embodiment, the charging station includes a bulk feedstock hopper coupled to a controllable dispensing mechanism. The dispensing mechanism may operate in a coarse feed mode and a fine feed mode. During coarse feed, material is dispensed at a relatively high rate until the tube weight approaches a target value. During fine feed, material is dispensed at a lower rate to achieve a final charge mass within a predetermined tolerance.

The charging station may include a tube receiving fixture configured to hold the recharge tube during filling. The fixture may align the upper opening of the tube with a dispensing outlet. A seal, bellows, sleeve, flexible coupling, or close-clearance interface may be provided between the dispensing outlet and the tube opening to reduce particle escape and contamination ingress.

The charging station may further include material flow sensors, level sensors, vibration sensors, imaging devices, and/or jam detection sensors. If a bridging condition, blockage, abnormal flow condition, or excessive particle generation is detected, the control system may pause dispensing and perform a corrective operation, such as vibrating the chute, pulsing a gate, reversing a feeder, or alerting an operator.

In certain embodiments, the charging station includes a material verification module configured to verify the type or lot of feedstock material before or during charging. Verification may be performed using barcode scanning of material containers, RFID identification, weight reconciliation, optical inspection, spectroscopy, conductivity measurement, or recipe comparison.

#### 5. Weighing Station

The weighing station may include one or more load cells, balances, force sensors, or scale assemblies configured to determine the weight of a recharge tube. The weighing station may be separate from the charging station or integrated with the charging station such that the tube is weighed while material is being dispensed.

In one embodiment, the weighing station measures the empty tube weight before charging and measures the loaded tube weight after charging. The control system subtracts the empty tube weight from the loaded tube weight to calculate the actual feedstock charge mass. In another embodiment, a stored tare weight associated with the tube identifier is used.

In a further embodiment, the charging station is mounted on or coupled to a weighing platform, thereby allowing real-time weight feedback during filling. The control system may operate a closed-loop dispensing algorithm based on weight readings. The algorithm may account for in-flight material, feeder response delay, vibration-induced noise, tube tare variation, and settling of feedstock material.

The weighing station may include calibration weights or an automatic calibration mechanism. The control system may periodically verify scale accuracy and may inhibit charging if calibration is overdue or if measured values deviate from expected ranges.

The system may compare measured charge mass against a target charge mass and a tolerance range. If the actual charge mass is below the lower tolerance limit, the system may add material. If the actual charge mass is above the upper tolerance limit, the system may reject the tube, route the tube to a correction station, or permit removal of material under controlled conditions.

#### 6. Transfer Module

The transfer module is configured to move recharge tubes between the storage module, charging station, weighing station, and one or more output or docking positions. The transfer module may include a robotic arm, gantry robot, linear shuttle, vertical lift, rotary indexer, automated guided vehicle, autonomous mobile robot, conveyor, overhead hoist, rail-guided cart, or combinations thereof.

The transfer module may include an end effector configured to grip or support a recharge tube. The end effector may engage a collar, flange, groove, handle, carrier frame, or tube body. The end effector may include clamps, vacuum pads, magnetic elements, expanding mandrels, hooks, fingers, or cradle supports. In some embodiments, the end effector includes sensors for confirming grip state, tube presence, tube orientation, cap engagement, and load.

The transfer module may be controlled to maintain the recharge tube in an orientation that prevents loss of feedstock material. For example, a charged tube may be maintained substantially vertical or may be tilted only within a predetermined angular range. The transfer module may include acceleration and vibration limits to reduce particle generation, material fracture, or shifting of the charge.

In one embodiment, the transfer module delivers a charged recharge tube to a docking interface of a crystal pulling apparatus. The docking interface may align the tube with a recharge port, load lock, transfer chamber, or feeder mechanism of the crystal puller. The system may include interlocks that prevent transfer unless the puller is ready to receive the tube, the tube identity matches the recipe, and environmental and safety conditions are satisfied.

In another embodiment, the transfer module places charged tubes into transport carriers. The carriers may then be moved to one or more crystal pullers by a separate factory transport system.

#### 7. Recharge Tube Closure and Cap Handling

The system may include a closure handling mechanism configured to install, remove, inspect, or verify caps or valves associated with the recharge tube. A cap may be removed before charging and reinstalled after charging. Alternatively, the tube may include a gate or valve that is opened for charging and closed for storage and transport.

The closure handling mechanism may include a robotic cap gripper, rotary actuator, linear actuator, torque sensor, cap storage nest, cap cleaning feature, and/or seal inspection device. The control system may verify proper closure before transfer of a charged tube. If a cap is missing, loose, misaligned, contaminated, or incompatible with the tube, transfer may be inhibited.

#### 8. Contamination Control Features

Because semiconductor feedstock materials require high purity, the system may include contamination control features. The storage, charging, weighing, and transfer areas may be enclosed within a housing. The housing may be supplied with filtered air or inert gas, such as nitrogen or argon. The housing may maintain positive pressure relative to the surrounding environment.

The system may include HEPA or ULPA filtration, laminar flow modules, exhaust ports, purge ports, particle counters, humidity sensors, oxygen sensors, pressure sensors, and temperature sensors. Portions of the system in contact with the feedstock material or recharge tube may be formed of low-contamination materials, such as quartz, silicon, silicon carbide, fluoropolymer, high-purity stainless steel, or coated metals.

The charging interface may reduce exposure of feedstock material to ambient air by using sealed or semi-sealed connections. The system may also include cleaning features, such as ionized air blow-off, vacuum extraction, wipe stations, brushless cleaning stations, purge gas nozzles, or ultrasonic cleaning interfaces.

#### 9. Control System

The control system may include one or more processors, programmable logic controllers, motion controllers, memory devices, input/output modules, communication interfaces, and software instructions configured to operate the system. The control system may coordinate station-to-station transfer, tube identification, material dispensing, weighing, data recording, safety interlocks, and communication with external equipment.

The control system may execute a recipe-based operation. A recipe may define:

- material type;
- target charge mass;
- acceptable mass tolerance;
- tube type;
- destination puller;
- charging sequence;
- feed rate parameters;
- purge requirements;
- cap handling requirements;
- allowable storage duration;
- environmental limits;
- transfer orientation limits; and
- process authorization conditions.

The control system may be connected to a manufacturing execution system, equipment automation system, supervisory control system, or crystal puller controller. Communication may be performed using SECS/GEM, Ethernet/IP, OPC UA, Modbus, PROFINET, fieldbus, wireless communication, or other industrial communication protocols.

The control system may maintain a database of tube status and material inventory. The database may allow traceability from feedstock material lot to recharge tube to crystal puller to ingot or wafer lot. The system may generate electronic records, alarms, reports, and audit trails.

#### 10. Exemplary Operating Method

An exemplary method of operating the recharge tube management system may include the following steps:

1. receiving a plurality of recharge tubes in a storage module;
2. identifying each recharge tube using a machine-readable identifier;
3. retrieving tube status information from a database;
4. selecting a recharge tube for charging based on a production recipe;
5. transferring the selected recharge tube from storage to a weighing station;
6. measuring an empty weight or confirming a stored tare weight;
7. transferring the tube to a charging station, or positioning the tube on an integrated weighing/charging station;
8. opening or removing a closure associated with the tube;
9. dispensing semiconductor feedstock material into the recharge tube;
10. measuring the tube weight during or after dispensing;
11. controlling the dispensing operation until a target charge mass is reached;
12. closing the tube;
13. verifying the final weight and closure state;
14. associating the final charge mass and material lot information with the tube identifier;
15. transferring the charged tube to a storage position, transport carrier, or crystal puller docking interface;
16. confirming acceptance of the charged tube by the destination equipment; and
17. updating the tube record and material inventory.

If the measured weight is outside a predetermined tolerance, the system may perform a corrective action, such as adding material, routing the tube to a correction station, generating an alarm, or marking the tube as unavailable for process use.

#### 11. Safety and Interlock Features

The system may include safety features for protecting operators, equipment, and process material. Safety features may include guarded enclosures, access doors with interlocks, emergency stop circuits, light curtains, area scanners, torque and force limiting drives, collision detection, load monitoring, and overtravel sensors.

The control system may inhibit motion of the transfer module when an access panel is open, when a tube is not properly gripped, when a charged tube is uncapped, when a destination port is unavailable, or when environmental conditions are outside allowable ranges. The system may also verify that the weight of a tube is within a safe handling range before transfer.

#### 12. Alternative Embodiments

In some embodiments, the weighing function is incorporated into the transfer mechanism. For example, a robotic end effector may include a load cell that weighs the tube while the robot holds the tube. In other embodiments, each storage position may include a load sensor so that the system can continuously monitor the weight or presence of stored tubes.

In further embodiments, the charging station may charge multiple tubes sequentially or simultaneously. A multi-position charging station may include an indexed turntable or linear array of tube fixtures. The system may select different feedstock sources to produce different charge recipes.

In another embodiment, the system includes a recharge tube conditioning station configured to preheat, cool, purge, dry, inspect, or clean recharge tubes before or after charging. A preheating feature may reduce thermal shock when material is introduced into a hot crystal pulling environment. A purge feature may remove oxygen, moisture, or airborne contaminants from the tube interior.

In still another embodiment, the system includes a simulation or scheduling module configured to determine when recharge tubes should be charged and delivered based on crystal puller status, melt level, ingot growth schedule, feedstock inventory, and production priority. The scheduling module may reduce waiting time and maintain availability of charged tubes.

#### 13. Advantages

The disclosed system may provide one or more advantages, including:

- automated handling of recharge tubes;
- improved charge mass accuracy;
- reduced operator exposure to heavy or fragile components;
- reduced contamination risk;
- enhanced traceability of feedstock material and recharge tubes;
- improved coordination with crystal pulling equipment;
- reduced process variation;
- automated verification of tube identity and status;
- reduced likelihood of using an incorrect material or tube;
- electronic record generation for quality control; and
- improved manufacturing throughput.

### Example Claim-Supporting Features

The technical features disclosed herein may support claims directed to, for example:

- an integrated recharge tube management system including storage, charging, weighing, and transfer modules;
- closed-loop dispensing of semiconductor feedstock material based on real-time tube weight;
- identification-based tracking of recharge tubes and associated material lots;
- automated verification of tube status before transfer to a crystal puller;
- contamination-controlled charging and storage of recharge tubes;
- robotic transfer of charged recharge tubes while maintaining orientation limits;
- cap or closure handling integrated with charging and transfer;
- database management of recharge tube history, weight, destination, and material information;
- interlocked docking between a recharge tube management system and a crystal puller; and
- scheduling and inventory management for multiple recharge tubes and multiple crystal pullers.
